[Music]  one hundred years ago this week a double  murder an hour outside new york city  became a front page sensation launching  the popularity of a new type of  newspaper called a tabloid  filled with intrigue lies and an illicit  affair the fascinating story is detailed  in a new book called blood and ink by  vanity fair writer joe pompeo  we sat down with pompeo in new brunswick  new jersey at the church where the  victims a reverend and a singer in his  choir  met for the first time  a century ago america was emerging from  years of war and pandemic  ready to roar into the 20s  in the 1920s i think people were looking  for things to  obsess over you know that were  you know didn't have to do with the war  they just come out of this pandemic that  had you know the period of the country i  mean it was coming back to life after  this really dark time and i think the  hall mills  story as well as other sensations of the  roaring 20s and the jazz age people just  loved following these things  this is the morning of september 16th  1922.  in a field near new brunswick new jersey  two locals discovered two bodies  a man and a woman  both had been shot  left down a secluded road  in a very specific way the bodies were  positioned next to one another in a pose  that clearly suggested some sort of  intimacy uh you know her his arm was  outstretched and her head rested on his  arm  her hand had been placed on his thigh  you know most telling of all there was a  stack of papers between them these were  love letters  love letters documenting the  relationship between the victims  both married but not to each other  one a prominent church leader the  reverend edward hall  the other a choir singer named eleanor  mills  we're sitting in the place where edward  and eleanor may have met  for the first time you know this is this  is certainly kind of the you know ground  zero of their love affair you know and  they would after choir practice they  would sometimes disappear to his his  study which is still you know there's a  room over there which is his study and  they would leave  their love notes in different in hymnals  or there was a book on his bookshelf i  mean all of a lot of the intrigue  happened in this very literally in the  very place where we're sitting right now  the case was full of sex scandal  and speculation  was the reverend's older wife and  heiress worth millions the one  responsible  was it her oddball brothers  was it eleanor mills husband who  certainly had his own motive  or someone else  the story was tailor-made for a whole  new brand of journalism  you know newspapers they've been  obsessed with crime and murder going  back to you know the penny press of the  victorian era and certainly the hearst  and the pulitzer papers and the yellow  press the turn of the century you know  people followed this stuff but this was  the first time there was a new  medium which is you know the tabloid  press covering things like this you know  with photographs one tabloid the  up-and-coming new york daily news went  so far as to try to elicit a confession  from eleanor mills husband  by staging a seance  as joe pompeo writes in his new book  blood and ink the scandalous jazz age  double murder that hooked america on  true crime this was the o.j simpson case  of its day you had about 300 reporters  from all over the country that were you  know trekking around you know all the  hotels are booked up they're renting out  houses  [Music]  despite unprecedented attention the case  lingered for years  until it was revived by another tabloid  police used the media attention  along with the accounts of a mysterious  local farmer known as the pig woman to  bring the case to trial  charging the wife of the reverend and  her brothers  in perhaps the most dramatic testimony  the country had ever seen  the pig woman deathly ill by then  was wheeled into a courtroom  on her hospital bed but at the very end  you know she's being wheeled back out  and she raises her voice and she looks  at francis hall in points and she says  i've told the truth and you know i've  told the truth so help me god it's like  if this was a movie  you couldn't make that line up  [Music]  when the trial was over frances hall and  her brothers were acquitted  and successfully sued the tabloid  but the story didn't end there  and in fact  never has  100 years later  will this crime ever be solved  i don't know what testing you could do  at this point to to determine anything  um there's a there's people who who very  much want to solve this crime these sort  of amateur detectives and historians  there's there's a facebook group where  you know this community of people who  are obsessed with this crime but i think  also with stories like this i think what  makes them so powerful is that we don't  know and i think that's you know to me  um i think that's what gives a story a  lot of it's of its power and mystique  and we want you to solve the case  jeffrey  i was trying to solve the case as i'm  reading the book and as i'm walking  around new brunswick right here um and  everybody does who approaches this story  because it does remain and it's hard to  imagine it ever being solved but now we  could  feel free to investigate it this was a  media circus it really was before media  circus yeah yeah one of the original  yeah um and um and the book is a  fascinating trip through through history  yes but it was on my yeah sounds like a  good one thanks jeff

